import AppKit

/// The selected action — the sixth piece of view state (spec §11 CI-1): nil,
/// or ONE action of the SINGLE selected clip. Like the other five it is not
/// undoable, not persisted and has no MCP mirror; agents address actions by
/// id through the action ops, which is the whole capability.
///
/// Three writers and no others: `selectAction(_:)` below (the lane, the
/// timeline's bands, esc, the Clip chip, an add), `syncSelectedAction()`
/// (the playhead ENTERING a span), and `clampViewState`, which drops an id
/// the edited project no longer has on the selected clip.
///
/// **Entry, not presence.** `lastInsideAction` is the span the playhead was
/// last inside, so a sync selects only on a CHANGE of span: after esc the
/// playhead can sit inside one without re-selecting it until it leaves and
/// comes back, and leaving a span keeps the selection. An edit never enters
/// one — `clampViewState` re-reads `lastInsideAction` before its own
/// playhead write, so a redo, a live drag or an add that puts a span under a
/// stationary playhead selects nothing.
///
/// The stored pair lives in `ProjectDocument.swift` because an extension
/// cannot add stored properties (`+DisplayStem`'s precedent for the split);
/// the queries and the entry rule live here, where the size rule wants them.
extension ProjectDocument {
    /// The edited project's clip when exactly one clip is selected.
    var singleSelectedClip: ClipSnapshot? {
        guard selection.count == 1, let id = selection.first else { return nil }
        return editedProject.snapshot.clip(id)
    }

    /// The single selected clip's action with `start ≤ clipTime(playhead) <
    /// endTime`. Actions on one clip never overlap, so there is at most one.
    var actionUnderPlayhead: ActionSnapshot? {
        guard let clip = singleSelectedClip else { return nil }
        let t = clip.clipTime(playhead)
        return clip.actions.first { $0.start <= t && t < $0.endTime }
    }

    /// The selected action's snapshot, read fresh from the edited project —
    /// nil when nothing is selected or the id is no longer the clip's.
    var selectedActionSnapshot: ActionSnapshot? {
        guard let id = selectedAction else { return nil }
        return singleSelectedClip?.actions.first { $0.id == id }
    }

    /// The entry rule. Called from `playhead`'s and `selection`'s `didSet`.
    func syncSelectedAction() {
        let under = actionUnderPlayhead?.id
        guard under != lastInsideAction else { return }
        lastInsideAction = under
        if let under { selectedAction = under }
    }

    /// The one deliberate writer. An id that is not the single selected
    /// clip's is stored as nil, so a multi-selection never holds one; the
    /// span under the playhead is recorded as already entered, so the next
    /// sync does not overrule the choice.
    func selectAction(_ id: UInt32?) {
        let owned = singleSelectedClip?.actions.contains { $0.id == id } == true
        selectedAction = owned ? id : nil
        lastInsideAction = actionUnderPlayhead?.id
    }
}

## How far the layout reaches

The layout is one layout switch at the camera clip's first frame, the same kind **Clip ▸ Camera Layout ▸** makes, so the screen keeps its full picture until the camera arrives. It ends with that stretch's screen clip or its camera clip, whichever is later.

That keeps it to this recording. A clip that was already in the project never moves, and another stretch's screen with no camera of its own stays full.

If a stretch's screen clip is missing, or isn't playing where its camera starts, that camera goes in the bottom-right corner instead, whichever layout you chose.

## Remove the camera's background

Tick **Remove the camera's background when it lands** and Frame Job runs [**Clip ▸ Remove Background**](/effects/remove-background/) on the camera's clips as soon as they land. It works on your Mac, with no key.

The box is off until you tick it, and Frame Job remembers it. It's greyed out, keeping its value, whenever **Layout** is.

It's its own undo step, **Remove Background**, after the landing's and before any [**Enhance the microphone when it lands**](/recording/when-it-lands/) and the **When it lands:** boxes. **Edit ▸ Undo** takes it off and keeps the recording. If no camera clip landed, it does nothing and says nothing.

## Change a camera clip afterwards

Each camera clip stays an ordinary clip. You can give it another layout from [**Clip ▸ Camera Layout ▸**](/framing/camera-layouts/) or the [Layout section](/framing/layout-section/).

The layout holds the camera in place, so dragging the clip alone doesn't move it. To make the camera bigger or smaller, or put it somewhere the layouts don't offer, see [Resize or move a laid-out camera](/framing/camera-layouts/#resize-or-move-a-laid-out-camera).

## Good to know

- If the camera stops on its own during the recording, for example because it's unplugged, the screen keeps recording. What the camera wrote lands, later stretches have no camera, and the panel's note gives the reason. If it stops while the recording is starting, the recording doesn't start.
- A camera file that would keep less than a frame after its trim stays in the [Media tab](/projects/media-tab/) without a clip, and a note says so. If no camera clip can be placed, no **Camera** track is added.
- If a stretch is left out because its movie can't be read, its camera file stays on disk and the note names it, but it isn't added to the project.

## With an agent

`record_screen` makes the same recording when it names a camera, and it can choose any of the five layouts and remove the camera's background. If no camera clip landed, the agent is told so. Landing in an open project, an agent can also leave the recording's files in the [Media tab](/projects/media-tab/) with no clips, which the panel doesn't offer. In that case no **Camera** track is added, and it can't ask for a layout or a removed background. See [Record with record_screen](/mcp/record-screen/#record-a-camera-beside-the-screen).

